<h1>Orcraft Test Flow</h1>
<p>A flow is an ordered sequence of steps with a shared result. It exists so a multi-step procedure can be
  named, reused across tests, and reported as a unit rather than as a run of unrelated actions.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTestFlow,
	OrcraftTestFlowConfig,
	OrcraftTestFlowResult,
	OrcraftConfigField,
	OrcraftResultField,
)


class MyFlowConfig(OrcraftTestFlowConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Flow")
	app_folder = OrcraftConfigField.PackageFolder(default="", label="App Folder")
	iterations = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=3, label="Iterations")


class MyFlowResult(OrcraftTestFlowResult):
	output_file = OrcraftResultField.Text(default="", label="Output File")
	measured_value = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=0, label="Measured Value")


class MyFlow(OrcraftTestFlow):
	result_type = MyFlowResult

	def __init__(self, config: MyFlowConfig) -> None:
		super().__init__(config)

	def _run_flow(self) -> None:
		first_result = self.run_step(
			MyFirstStep,
			app_folder=self.config.app_folder,
		)

		if self.handle_errors(first_result, self.result):
			return

		second_result = self.run_step(
			MySecondStep,
			input_file=first_result.output_file,
			iterations=self.config.iterations,
		)

		if self.handle_errors(second_result, self.result):
			return

		self.result.output_file = first_result.output_file
		self.result.measured_value = second_result.measured_value

		self.checkpoint(
			second_result.measured_value > 0,
			f"Measured {second_result.measured_value}.",
			"The system under test reported no measurement.",
		)') }}

<h2 id="result-type">Declaring the result class</h2>
<p>A test or a step builds its result inside the body. A flow names its result class up front instead,
  through the <code>result_type</code> class attribute, as <code>MyFlow</code> does above. The framework
  instantiates that class when the flow starts, so <code>self.result</code> exists and is ready to be
  filled in from the first line of <code>_run_flow</code> - which is also why <code>_run_flow</code>
  returns nothing: it populates <code>self.result</code> as it goes, and the caller reads that object
  back from <code>flow.run()</code>.</p>

<p>The attribute is optional. Omit it and the flow reports a plain <code>OrcraftTestFlowResult</code> -
  status, descriptions and timing, with no fields of its own - which is all a flow needs when it only
  sequences steps and has nothing of its own to measure.</p>

<h3 id="result-type-errors">Why the framework builds it: error reporting</h3>
<p>When a flow body raises, it is the framework that finishes the result. It sets the status to
  <code>ERROR</code>, records the error description, stamps the finish and elapsed times, and emits the
  result the controller renders. All of that lands on whatever <code>self.result</code> is <em>at that
  moment</em>. Building the declared class before the body runs guarantees that an early failure still
  reports through the flow's own result class: the log table and the controller show the flow's declared
  fields alongside the error, and its result columns do not vanish from the run.</p>

<p>The alternative is to do what a step does and assign the result inside the body:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('def _run_flow(self) -> None:
	self.result = MyFlowResult()
	...') }}

<p>On the happy path the two are identical - same class, same values, same result columns. They differ
  only when the body raises <em>before</em> that assignment line runs. The framework then finishes the
  base <code>OrcraftTestFlowResult</code> it created on its own: status and error text are still
  correct, but the flow's declared fields are absent and its result columns are lost for that run.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Situation</th>
      <th><code>result_type</code></th>
      <th>Assigned in the body</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Body completes</td>
      <td>Declared fields reported</td>
      <td>Declared fields reported</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Body raises after the assignment</td>
      <td>Declared fields reported</td>
      <td>Declared fields reported</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Body raises before the assignment</td>
      <td>Declared fields reported, empty</td>
      <td>Base result only - columns lost</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Put plainly, <code>result_type</code> turns "the result always carries the flow's declared shape"
  into a framework guarantee. Assigning in the body turns it into an authoring rule - make the assignment
  the first statement - that costs nothing when followed and quietly degrades error output when it is
  not.</p>

<h2 id="run-step">Running steps</h2>
<p><code>run_step</code> takes a step class, keyword values for that step's config fields, and optionally
  the endpoint to run it on:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('result = self.run_step(
	MySecondStep,
	endpoint=self.config.endpoint_2,
	input_file=first_result.output_file,
)') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Argument</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>step_method</code></td>
      <td>The step class, or an already-constructed step instance.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>endpoint</code></td>
      <td>Where to run it. Defaults to the endpoint the test is configured for.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>expect_endpoint_disconnect</code></td>
      <td>Set for a step that is meant to take its endpoint offline.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>**kwargs</code></td>
      <td>Values applied to the step config's fields.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Passing a keyword the step config does not declare raises immediately, naming the config and the
  field. A renamed field is caught at the call site rather than silently ignored, which is the failure
  mode a dictionary of parameters would have given you instead.</p>

<h2 id="short-circuit">Stopping on failure</h2>
<p>Steps in a flow are ordered because each one depends on the last. Two mechanisms keep a flow from
  running past a broken precondition.</p>

<p>The first is explicit:
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/results-and-statuses/#handle-errors"><code>handle_errors</code></a>
  after each step, copying the step result into the flow result and returning <code>True</code> when the
  flow should stop.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('step_result = self.run_step(MyFirstStep, app_folder=self.config.app_folder)

if self.handle_errors(step_result, self.result):
	return') }}

<p>The second is automatic: once the flow result is in <code>ERROR</code>, further <code>run_step</code>
  calls short-circuit instead of executing. A flow that loses its first step does not go on to run the
  rest against a state that never got set up.</p>

<p>Note that this applies to <code>ERROR</code>, not to <code>FAIL</code>. A failing checkpoint is a
  finding, and a flow keeps going so it can report the rest of them; an error means the flow cannot
  meaningfully continue.</p>

<h2 id="composing">Calling flows from a case</h2>
<p>A case body builds a flow, runs it, and reads its result:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> OrcraftTestResult:
	case.result = MyTestResult()

	flow = MyFlow(
		MyFlowConfig(
			app_folder=self.config.app_folder,
			iterations=case.params["iterations"],
		)
	)
	flow_result = flow.run()

	if self.handle_errors(flow_result, case.result):
		return case.result

	case.result.measured_value = flow_result.measured_value
	return case.result') }}

<p>Values move down through the flow config and back up through the flow result. A case that runs three
  flows reads as three named phases, and the log nests accordingly - test, case, flow, step - so a
  failure is located rather than searched for.</p>

<h2 id="endpoint-helpers">Endpoint helpers</h2>
<p>Flows also carry the helpers for driving endpoints, since the flow is the level that knows which
  endpoint each step is going to:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Method</th>
      <th>Purpose</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>wait_for_endpoint_state(state, endpoint=, timeout_sec=)</code></td>
      <td>Block until an endpoint reaches a given <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#state">state</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>restart_endpoint(endpoint=, ...)</code></td>
      <td>Restart an endpoint and wait for it to come back.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>transfer(paths, src_endpoint=, dst_endpoint=)</code></td>
      <td>Move files or a folder tree from one endpoint to another.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>All three are covered in <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/">Orcraft Endpoints</a>.</p>
